.TH MV 1.SH NAMEmv \- rename files, or move them into a directory.SH SYNOPSIS\fBmv\fR[\-bfiuv] [\-S suffix] [\-V method] source dest.br\fBmv\fR[\-bfiuv] [\-S suffix] [\-V method] source ... directory.SH DESCRIPTION\fBmv\fRis GNU mv, from fileutils 3.13.  Given two names it renames the first tothe second; given a directory last, it moves every other file named intoit, keeping their names.  Nothing is copied: the directory entry moves..SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-v\fRname each file as it moves:\fBm1 -> m2\fR.\fRLong form\fB+verbose\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-i\fRask before replacing a file.  Long form\fB+interactive\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-f\fRnever ask.  Long form\fB+force\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-u\fRmove only when the source is newer than the file it would replace.Long form\fB+update\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-b\fRkeep a backup of a file that would be replaced.  Long form\fB+backup\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-S\fI suffix\fRthe backup's suffix, `~' unless changed..TP 12\fB\-V\fI method\fRhow backups are named:\fBnumbered\fR,\fR\fBexisting\fRor\fBsimple\fR.\fR.SH EXAMPLES.nfmv -v draft final        rename, and say somv a.c b.c src           move two files into src.fi.SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fROn this disk\fBmv\fRdoes not replace a file that already exists: it reports the two files as`the same file' and stops with status 1, so\fB\-i\fR,\fR\fB\-b\fRand\fB\-u\fRhave nothing to act on.  Renaming to a new name and moving into adirectory both work..PPYour own OS-9 may have a utility of this name; see\fBDOC/README-NAMES\fR.\fR.SH SEE ALSOrm, cp